#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097b8a1b-b44d-5e76-97c8-b4c1a59a0e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345426} {:answer/id #uuid "6baff456-609b-5210-afb9-ca38e5adabcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346284} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc14084-3088-5b8a-ba2d-7b0e0a1aadc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346981} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb60f3a-a601-548c-bc1f-20c9cc148f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347151} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc15575-a017-565f-8f76-b1cadb4fc7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbc14084-3088-5b8a-ba2d-7b0e0a1aadc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346981} :answer-field/id #uuid "458244de-522e-5387-a418-9f5ed1cbeef6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e25ee2d5-6236-5cb7-ae46-36798fde6f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-79334" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,\\times $\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ we must have that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Argand Diagram Corresponding to a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c1e4021-3396-5bc6-83ea-5a27c6cabe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345596} {:answer/id #uuid "3dea748a-d0d4-5afc-925f-273d667f091e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345875} {:answer/id #uuid "7243bf19-b992-547d-9233-c657639a06c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346361} {:answer/id #uuid "d13f5329-2ec1-5018-9bce-9ec236dfe908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347185} {:answer/id #uuid "d7dc2a08-b945-5e4e-9cd1-679eca7a90c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13f5329-2ec1-5018-9bce-9ec236dfe908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347185} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced0da57-29f7-5224-b3bb-c98987c02847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8289966f-f636-5804-9edf-c054d1b5acd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79457" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in the corresponding Argand diagram?\n\n- $-2-2\\text{i}$\n- $-2+\\text{i}$\n- $-1+2\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $-2-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,-2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-2+\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,1)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-1+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-1$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-1,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n![](images/9f/9f2e7234ba52d14098e0ecfc9695181b425764c110995b76b4b0efddf206252c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Argand Diagram Corresponding to a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d4636db-db02-5863-92b4-8abb34eb2771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346581} {:answer/id #uuid "a4bb8bff-0b5e-51a9-8760-878220ba9463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346788} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f68a00-1742-54f4-9473-c700f4f12b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347028} {:answer/id #uuid "ca930a2a-126d-5edd-aa68-a071673eb20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347117} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c0088a-60c2-538f-9ba7-d71e584516e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca930a2a-126d-5edd-aa68-a071673eb20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347117} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd69c411-1af3-5cc9-9bc9-5772e6c3d56f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1861228f-d3da-56c4-af5a-dc5d012713d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79458" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the fourth quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$\n- $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{3}{2},-1)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$, which lies in the first quadrant.\n\n![](images/d7/d7390ddcc8d338d2fd46bc12b0a4c65f8e6bf6e8c4f6c9fa802e2acea159416b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}]}
